  'html' => '<h1>Completing a Case &amp; Closing Out</h1>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Who this is for:</strong> Project Managers doing final inspections, Caseworkers coordinating sign-off, and Bursars reconciling finances.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Why This Is Final</h2>
<p>Proper closure ensures legal protection, grant compliance, and happy applicants. A case that isn\'t closed properly can come back to haunt you — literally and financially.</p>
<p><strong>An open case with completed work is a liability.</strong> Funders ask questions. Applicants get confused. Data gets messy. Close it right.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 1: Final Inspection Checklist</h2>
<p>Before closing a case, the Project Manager should verify:</p>
<h3>Work Completion</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] <strong>All tasks in the project are marked Complete</strong></li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Each task has photos</strong> — before and after, clearly labeled</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Hours are logged</strong> — volunteer and paid hours both</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Materials are recorded</strong> — what was used, what cost</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Quality checks passed</strong> — no rework needed</li>
</ul>
<h3>Property Condition</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] <strong>Worksite is clean</strong> — no debris, tools, or materials left behind</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>All repairs match scope</strong> — nothing missed, nothing extra (unless change-ordered)</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>No new damage</strong> — the repair didn\'t create new problems</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Utilities are functional</strong> — water, electricity, HVAC all working</li>
</ul>
<h3>Documentation</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] <strong>After photos uploaded</strong> — same angles as before photos</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Assessment referenced</strong> — the original assessment is linked to the completed work</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Progress events logged</strong> — the timeline tells the full story</li>
<li>[ ] <strong>Change orders documented</strong> — any scope changes have approval records</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Step 2: Applicant Sign-Off</h2>
<p>The applicant should confirm they\'re satisfied with the work:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Walk through the repairs</strong> with the applicant</li>
<li><strong>Explain what was done</strong> — in plain language, not construction jargon</li>
<li><strong>Ask for sign-off</strong> — a verbal confirmation at minimum, a signature if your program requires it</li>
<li><strong>Address any concerns</strong> — if the applicant isn\'t satisfied, note the issue and assign a corrective task</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>What if the applicant isn\'t happy?</strong> Don\'t close the case. Assign a follow-up task, fix the issue, and do another walkthrough. A closed case with an unhappy applicant is a complaint waiting to happen.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>Certificate of Completion</h3>
<p>Some programs issue a Certificate of Completion to the applicant. This typically includes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Household name and address</li>
<li>Date of completion</li>
<li>Description of work performed</li>
<li>Organization name</li>
<li>Signature of PM or caseworker</li>
</ul>
<p>This is a nice touch for the applicant and useful for grant documentation.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 3: Upload Final After-Photos</h2>
<p>After-photos should:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Match before-photo angles</strong> — same position, same lighting if possible</li>
<li><strong>Show the full repair area</strong> — wide shot plus close-up</li>
<li><strong>Be clearly labeled</strong> — "After - Roof repair, east section"</li>
<li><strong>Be uploaded to the case</strong> — not just stored on someone\'s phone</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Grant reporting tip:</strong> Funders love before-and-after comparisons. Good photos are the difference between "they said they did the work" and "here\'s the proof."</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Step 4: Reconcile Finances</h2>
<p>The Bursar should:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Review all invoices</strong> — subcontractor bills, material receipts</li>
<li><strong>Compare to budget</strong> — did the project stay within the allocated amount?</li>
<li><strong>Process final payments</strong> — any outstanding invoices get paid</li>
<li><strong>Record expenses</strong> — all costs logged against the case</li>
<li><strong>Generate a financial summary</strong> — budgeted vs. actual, by category</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>If the project went over budget:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Document why (change order? under-estimate? unexpected issue?)</li>
<li>Get admin approval for the overage</li>
<li>Note the reason in the case file</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>If the project came in under budget:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Document the savings</li>
<li>The excess returns to the organization\'s funding pool</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Financial accuracy matters.</strong> Grant auditors will look at individual case costs. Every dollar needs a receipt and a reason.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Step 5: Close the Case in the System</h2>
<p>When everything above is done:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Open the case</strong> in your dashboard</li>
<li><strong>Verify all projects are marked Complete</strong> — a case can have multiple projects; all must be done</li>
<li><strong>Set case status to "Concluded"</strong> — this signals the case is fully closed</li>
<li><strong>Log the closing event</strong> — "Case concluded on [date] — all repairs completed, applicant satisfied"</li>
<li><strong>Confirm the application status</strong> — if ALL repair needs across ALL cases are met, the Application status should also update to "Concluded"</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>Step 6: Post-Completion Follow-Up</h2>
<p><strong>30 days after closure:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Contact the applicant to confirm repairs are holding up</li>
<li>Note any issues — roof still leaking? Plumbing working?</li>
<li>If problems are found, the case can be <strong>reopened</strong> (see below)</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>90 days after closure:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Final check-in (programs vary on whether this is required)</li>
<li>Close the loop with the funder if they require post-completion reporting</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>When Cases Get Reopened</h2>
<p>Sometimes a case needs to come back to life. Common reasons:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Reason</th>
<th>What Happens</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Repair failure</strong> — the roof leaks again</td>
<td>Reopen case, assign corrective task, document the failure</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>New damage</strong> — a storm causes additional damage</td>
<td>This may be a NEW application, not a reopen</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Grant audit finding</strong> — funder questions costs</td>
<td>Reopen case, gather documentation, respond to funder</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Applicant complaint</strong> — something wasn\'t done right</td>
<td>Reopen case, assess the complaint, fix if warranted</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Reopening a case:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>Change status from "Concluded" back to "Active"</li>
<li>Log the reopening reason</li>
<li>Assign corrective tasks</li>
<li>Set a timeline for resolution</li>
<li>Close again when resolved</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>Archiving Case Documents</h2>
<p>Once a case is concluded and the follow-up period has passed:</p>
<ul>
<li>All documents should be <strong>properly filed</strong> in the case record</li>
<li>Photos should be <strong>organized</strong> (before/after by repair type)</li>
<li>Financial records should be <strong>complete</strong> (receipts, invoices, payments)</li>
<li>Communications should be <strong>logged</strong> (all interactions in the system)</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Retention periods vary by funder and program.</strong> Common requirements:</p>
<ul>
<li>Financial records: 7 years</li>
<li>Photos: permanent</li>
<li>Contracts: 10 years</li>
<li>General case files: 5 years</li>
</ul>
